# ISRG Root YR, cross-signed by ISRG Root X1.
#
# Why this file exists: AMS-IX serves https://stats.ams-ix.net/exports/members.json
# behind a Let's Encrypt certificate whose chain terminates at "ISRG Root YR", a
# new Let's Encrypt root that is NOT yet in certifi (checked against 2026.7.22,
# the latest release) nor in the macOS system trust store. The chain AMS-IX
# serves is complete and correctly configured -- our trust store is simply behind.
# That one URL serves 14 AMS-IX exchanges and 1,207 participants, Amsterdam's 910
# among them, so it is the single most costly export to lose.
#
# This is the CROSS-SIGNED form: subject "ISRG Root YR", issuer "ISRG Root X1".
# X1 is already in certifi, so this certificate's provenance is verifiable rather
# than taken on faith -- `openssl verify -CAfile $(python -c 'import certifi;
# print(certifi.where())') isrg-root-yr-cross-signed.pem` returns OK. Downloaded
# from https://letsencrypt.org/certs/gen-y/root-yr-by-x1.pem
# SHA-256: 07:26:39:D0:B1:40:D5:BF:FA:E1:6A:D9:C3:F6:CC:60:86:04:06:21:F5:1E:E6:1A:6D:46:A8:91:5C:07:CF:76
#
# It is used ONLY for the EuroIX export fetches (see snapshot.py::_ca_bundle),
# never globally. DELETE THIS FILE once certifi ships ISRG Root YR.
